=== layout: one-panel

# *Three Types of H-W Problems*

@body

- Type 1\:
  - Given all allele frequencies, p and q
  - Calculate expected genotype frequencies.
- Type 2\:
  - Given one or more genotype frequencies, p^2^
  - Calculate allele frequencies.
- Type 3\:
  - Given all genotype frequencies,
  - Determine whether or not population is evolving
  - uses Chi-Square

=== layout: section

# Wildflower Example

=== layout: two-panels

# *Wildflower Example*

@left

- For example, imagine a population of 500 wildflower plants with two alleles, C^R^ and C^W^, for a locus that codes for flower pigment.
  - These alleles show incomplete dominance; thus, each genotype has a distinct phenotype.
- Incomplete dominance\:
  - Plants homozygous for the C^R^ allele (C^R^C^R^) produce red pigment and have [red flowers]{color=red};
  - plants homozygous for the C^W^ allele (C^W^C^W^) produce no red pigment and have [white flowers]{color=gray};
  - and heterozygotes (C^R^C^W^) produce some red pigment and have [pink flowers]{color=#EC9BA4}.

@right

![Slide image 2](assets/lect14c-hardy_weinberg_1/image_002.png)

=== layout: two-panels

# *Allele Frequency*

@left

- Each allele has a frequency (proportion) in the population.
- For example, suppose our population has\:
  - [320 plants with red flowers]{color=red}
  - [160 with pink flowers]{color=#EC9BA4}
  - [20 with white flowers]{color=gray}
- Because these are diploid organisms, these 500 individuals have a total of 1,000 copies of the gene for flower color.

@right

![Slide image 2](assets/lect14c-hardy_weinberg_1/image_002.png)

=== layout: two-panels

# *Allele Counts*

@left

- For example, consider a population of wildflowers that is incompletely dominant for color
  - [320 red flowers (C^R^C^R^)]{color=red}
  - [160 pink flowers (C^R^C^W^)]{color=#EC9BA4}
  - [20 white flowers (C^W^C^W^)]{color=gray}
- Calculate the number of copies of each allele
  - C^R^ = (320 &#215; 2) + 160 = 800
  - C^W^ = (20 &#215; 2) + 160 = 200

@right

![Slide image 2](assets/lect14c-hardy_weinberg_1/image_002.png)

=== layout: one-panel

# *Allele Frequency*

@body

![Slide image 3](assets/lect14c-hardy_weinberg_1/image_003.png)

- To calculate the frequency of each allele
  - p = freq C^R^ = 800 / (800 + 200) = 0.8
  - q = freq C^W^ = 200 / (800 + 200) = 0.2
- The sum of alleles is always 1
  - 0.8 + 0.2 = 1

=== layout: one-panel


@body

![Slide image 4](assets/lect14c-hardy_weinberg_1/image_004.png)

